      "transcript": "# Binary search halves the range at every step\n\nBinary search looks for 23 in the sorted list 2, 5, 8, 12, 16, 23, 38, 56, 72. It compares with the middle element, keeps the half that can still hold 23, and finds it in three comparisons. A million items need at most twenty comparisons.\n\n**00:00 Find 23 in a sorted list.** We look for 23 in a sorted list of nine numbers.\n\n**00:05 Compare with the middle.** Look at the middle element. If it equals the target, stop. If the target is larger, keep the right half. Otherwise, keep the left half.\n\n**00:15 Where the middle is.** The middle index, mid, is low plus high, divided by two, rounded down. Low and high mark the range that is still possible.\n\n**00:24 Three comparisons find 23.** Mid is index 4, value 16, smaller than 23, so low becomes 5. Mid is index 6, value 38, larger, so high becomes 5. Mid is index 5: found.\n\n**00:36 Steps grow slowly with size.** Every comparison halves the range. A thousand items need at most ten comparisons, and a million need at most twenty.\n\n**00:45 What binary search needs.** This works only on sorted data with fast access to any index, such as an array.\n\nSources:\n\n- Source binary-search-source — https://example.com/binary-search-source\n",
      "explanation": "Each comparison with the middle element removes half of the remaining range, so the number of steps grows with the logarithm of the list size, not the size itself.",
      "alt": "Binary search looks for 23 in the sorted list 2, 5, 8, 12, 16, 23, 38, 56, 72. It compares with the middle element, keeps the half that can still hold 23, and finds it in three comparisons. A million items need at most twenty comparisons."
    },
